"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,351\nEpisode 3\n\n3\n00:00:07,431 --> 00:00:09,233\nDepth and meaning\n\n4\n00:00:12,033 --> 00:00:14,043\nThree questions about the same word.\n\n5\n00:00:14,508 --> 00:00:16,038\nIs it in capital letters?\n\n6\n00:00:16,321 --> 00:00:17,911\nDoes it rhyme with train?\n\n7\n00:00:18,232 --> 00:00:22,512\nWould it fit the sentence: the student used her blank to solve the problem?\n\n8\n00:00:23,019 --> 00:00:24,717\nOn a surprise test later,\n\n9\n00:00:24,907 --> 00:00:28,916\npeople recognized about one in seven words they had judged for case,\n\n10\n00:00:29,112 --> 00:00:30,869\nabout half the rhyme words,\n\n11\n00:00:30,974 --> 00:00:34,833\nand about four in five of the words they had fitted into a sentence.\n\n12\n00:00:35,003 --> 00:00:36,487\nNothing else changed.\n\n13\n00:00:36,861 --> 00:00:39,491\nThe same words appeared in every condition,\n\n14\n00:00:39,718 --> 00:00:41,951\nand nobody was trying to memorize them.\n\n15\n00:00:42,497 --> 00:00:45,669\nWhat people were asked to think about decided what stayed.\n\n16\n00:00:46,178 --> 00:00:54,012\nThis is the levels of processing idea: processing for meaning leaves a more durable trace than processing for surface.\n\n17\n00:00:54,182 --> 00:00:57,025\nTrying to remember matters less than you might expect.\n\n18\n00:00:57,412 --> 00:00:59,267\nWhen people were warned about the test,\n\n19\n00:00:59,488 --> 00:01:02,668\nmemory for case words rose only to about a quarter,\n\n20\n00:01:02,948 --> 00:01:05,690\nand meaning words stayed at about eighty percent.\n\n21\n00:01:06,295 --> 00:01:12,923\nIn earlier studies, rating words for pleasantness produced recall about as good as deliberately studying them.\n\n22\n00:01:13,376 --> 00:01:16,075\nCounting the letter e in each word did not.\n\n23\n00:01:16,245 --> 00:01:17,913\nDepth is not just more time,\n\n24\n00:01:17,933 --> 00:01:18,492\neither.\n\n25\n00:01:19,007 --> 00:01:26,166\nA slow, fiddly task about the pattern of consonants and vowels took longer than an easy question about meaning,\n\n26\n00:01:26,556 --> 00:01:28,516\nand still produced worse memory.\n\n27\n00:01:28,686 --> 00:01:29,806\nTwo cautions.\n\n28\n00:01:30,135 --> 00:01:33,711\nDepth has never had a clean measure apart from memory itself,\n\n29\n00:01:33,893 --> 00:01:36,176\nwhich makes the idea partly circular.\n\n30\n00:01:36,577 --> 00:01:38,501\nAnd deep is not always best.\n\n31\n00:01:38,895 --> 00:01:40,992\nIf the test asks about sound,\n\n32\n00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:42,784\nprocessing sound can win.\n\n33\n00:01:43,395 --> 00:01:47,801\nWhat matters is the match between how something is learned and how it is used,\n\n34\n00:01:48,065 --> 00:01:50,352\nwhich is where episode ten picks up.\n\n35\n00:01:50,522 --> 00:01:51,296\nFor teaching.\n\n36\n00:01:51,649 --> 00:01:54,198\nLook at what your activities make students think about.\n\n37\n00:01:54,557 --> 00:01:57,200\nHighlighting and copying keep attention on the surface.\n\n38\n00:01:57,220 --> 00:02:00,586\nInstead, ask for judgments about meaning: why does this step work?\n\n39\n00:02:00,782 --> 00:02:01,979\nHow is this case like the last one?\n\n40\n00:02:02,383 --> 00:02:04,249\nWhich example would break the rule?\n\n41\n00:02:04,525 --> 00:02:07,385\nHave students sort, compare,\n\n42\n00:02:07,405 --> 00:02:10,668\nand explain, rather than transcribe.\n\n43\n00:02:11,041 --> 00:02:13,955\nAnd be wary of tasks that are busy but shallow.\n\n44\n00:02:14,125 --> 00:02:16,317\nMemory is a by-product of thinking.\n\n45\n00:02:16,692 --> 00:02:18,995\nWhat students think about is what they keep.\n\n"
